            "output": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_92a77625568c1b440bc663fafc426a65\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://research.nvidia.com/sites/default/files/pubs/2010-01_Fast-Tridiagonal-Solvers/Zhang_Fast_2009.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"[PDF] Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU - Research at NVIDIA\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2009-11-02\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU\\nYao Zhang\\nUniversity of California, Davis yaozhang@ucdavis.edu\\nJonathan Cohen\\nNVIDIA jocohen@nvidia.com\\nJohn D. Owens\\nUniversity of California, Davis jowens@ece.ucdavis.edu\\nAbstract\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recur- sive doubling (RD).\\nWe develop an approach to measure, analyze, and optimize the performance of GPU programs in terms of mem- ory access, computation, and control overhead. We find that CR en- joys linear algorithm complexity but suffers from more algorithmic steps and bank conflicts, while PCR and RD have fewer algorith- mic steps but do more work each step.\\nTo combine the benefits of the basic algorithms, we propose hybrid CR+PCR and CR+RD al- gorithms, which improve the performance of PCR, RD and CR by 21%, 31% and 61% respectively. Our GPU solvers achieve up to a 28x speedup over a sequential LAPACK solver, and a 12x speedup over a multi-threaded CPU solver.\\nThis is in contrast to a traditional bottleneck GPU performance model, in which only the improvement on the bottleneck leads to overall per- formance increase. By applying the above analysis approach to optimizing tridi- agonal solvers, we are able to achieve a significant speedup with hybrid algorithms that we would not have achieved if we stayed\\nWe propose hybrid CR+PCR and CR+RD algorithms and show how they achieve bet- ter performance by reducing the number of inefficient algorithmic steps, avoiding bank conflicts, and improving the utilization of vec- tor hardware.\\nThe hybrid algorithms improve PCR, RD and CR by 21%, 31% and 61% respectively, and achieve a 12.5x speedup over a multi-threaded CPU solver. Third, we perform experiments to determine the accuracy of various algorithms and give sugges- tions for improving numerical stability.\\nFor solving 512 512-unknown systems, the hybrid solvers achieve a 12.5x speedup over the multi-threaded CPU solver, and a 28x speedup over the LAPACK solver. We recommend that the GPU solvers should be used as a part of a larger GPU applica- tion to amortize the PCI-Express transfer cost.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://research.nvidia.com/publication/2010-01_fast-tridiagonal-solvers-gpu\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU | Research\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"1. Publications\\n2. Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU\\nFast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recursive doubling (RD).\\nWe develop an approach to measure, analyze, and optimize the performance of GPU programs in terms of memory access, computation, and control overhead.We find that CR enjoys linear algorithm complexity but suffers from more algorithmic steps and bank conflicts, while PCR and RD have fewer algorithmic steps but do more work each step.\\nTo combine the benefits of the basic algorithms, we propose hybrid CR+PCR and CR+RD algorithms, which improve the performance of PCR, RD and CR by 21%, 31% and 61% respectively. Our GPU solvers achieve up to a 28x speedup over a sequential LAPACK solver, and a 12x speedup over a multi-threaded CPU solver.\\nAuthors\\nYao Zhang (UC Davis)\\nJonathan Cohen (NVIDIA)\\nJohn Owens (UC Davis)\\nPublication Date\\nFriday, January 1, 2010\\nPublished in\\n15th ACM SIGPLAN Symposium on Principles and Practice of Parallel Programming (…\\nResearch Area\\nAlgorithms and Numerical Methods\\nUploaded Files\\nZhang_Fast_2009.pdf1.32 MB\\nCopyright\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/1693453.1693472\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | Proceedings of the 15th ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Fast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | Proceedings of the 15th ...\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recursive doubling (RD).\\nFast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | Proceedings of the 15th ...\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recursive doubling (RD).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/algorithmic-step\",\n      \"title\": \"Algorithmic Step - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"A Hybrid Method for Solving Tridiagonal Systems on the GPU\\n2012, GPU Computing Gems Jade Edition Yao Zhang, ... John D. Owens\\n11.2Core Method\\nWe first characterize the complexity of three tridiagonal algorithms in terms of algorithmic steps and work per step as shown in Table 11.1 . The Thomas algorithm is essentially sequential Gaussian elimination, which requires the most algorithmic steps, but the least amount of work per step.\\nCyclic reduction (CR) consists of 2 log 2 n algorithmic steps, and can perform up to n /2 parallel units of work per step. Parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) requires the fewest algorithmic steps and the most parallel units of work per step.\\nTable 11.1 . Complexity Comparison of Algorithms in Terms of Algorithmic Steps and Work per Step.\\nThere are two variants of hybrid algorithm, CR-PCR and PCR-Thomas. Sengupta et al. use a similar idea to combine a step-efficient scan algorithm with a work-efficient one for implementation on a GPU [9] .\\nBook 2012, GPU Computing Gems Jade Edition Yao Zhang, ... John D. Owens\\nChapter\\nA Hybrid Method for Solving Tridiagonal Systems on the GPU\\n2012, GPU Computing Gems Jade Edition Yao Zhang, ... John D. Owens\\n11.3Algorithms\\nIn this section, we review three basic algorithms: the Thomas algorithm, CR, and PCR, and their two hybrid variants: CR-PCR and PCR-Thomas.\\n11.3.1The Thomas Algorithm\\nThe Thomas algorithm performs 8 n operations while CR performs 17 n operations. However, on a parallel computer with n processors, CR requires 2 log 2 n steps while the Thomas algorithm requires 2 n steps.\\nPCR is a variant of CR. In contrast to CR, PCR only has the forward reduction phase. Although the reduction mechanism and formula are the same as those of CR, in each reduction step, PCR reduces each of the current systems to two systems of half size.\\nFor example, for an 8-equation system as shown in Figure 11.3 , PCR first reduces it from one 8-equation system to two 4-equation systems in step 1, from two 4-equation systems to four 2-equation systems in step 2, from four 2-equation systems to eight 1-equation systems in step 3, and finally solves eight 1-equation systems.\\nPCR takes 12 n log 2 n operations and log 2 n steps to finish. PCR requires fewer algorithmic steps than CR but does asymptotically more work per step.\\nFigure 11.3 . Communication pattern of PCR for an 8-equation system, showing the dataflow between each equation, labeled e 1 to e 8. Letters e ′, e ″ and e ‴ stand for updated equation.\\nThe performance gain is best illustrated in Figure 37.5 by highlighting the distinction between the compute-intensive phase (black bar) and the communication-intensive phase (white bar). The compute-intensive phase achieves 17.7× speedup on the GPU, while the communication-intensive phase achieves only 3.7× speedup on the GPU.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3716171\",\n      \"title\": \"High Performance Solution of Tridiagonal Systems on the GPU | ACM Transactions on Parallel Computing\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2025-05-13\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"High Performance Solution of Tridiagonal Systems on the GPU\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\n* 2 Tridiagonal Matrix Solvers\\n* 2.1 Thomas Algorithm\\n* 2.1.1 Data-Parallel Thomas Algorithm.\\n* 2.2 Parallel Cyclic Reduction (PCR) Algorithm\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nthe ratio of the off-diagonal to diagonal terms) and demonstrates that PfSolve achieves a considerable speedup over vendor solutions on modern HPC\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nThe fast evaluation of tridiagonal solutions is fundamental to calculate spectral expansions with the polynomial connection algorithm [ 14 ] and is\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nIn this article, we will present PfSolve —a fast, open-source, and cross-platform implementation of a tridiagonal solver based on the runtime\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nWe will demonstrate that our implementation achieves a considerable speedup over vendor solutions depending on the architecture and matrix size for\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\n2 Tridiagonal Matrix Solvers\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nA diagonal + second sub and super diagonal solver is essentially a tridiagonal solver of two independent systems of size (\\\\frac{N}{2}) , so no new\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nIn this section, we will showcase the performance of the PTA in three scenarios: single-upload tridiagonal solver with varying number of RHS vectors,\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nThe base scenario for a tridiagonal solver is a system of multiple RHS vectors solved against a single matrix using the algorithm from Section 2.3 .\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/1837853.1693472\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | ACM SIGPLAN Notices\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Fast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | ACM SIGPLAN Notices\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU:\\nFast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | ACM SIGPLAN Notices\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recursive doubling (RD).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.1145/3716171\",\n      \"title\": \"High Performance Solution of Tridiagonal Systems on the GPU | ACM Transactions on Parallel Computing\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2025-05-13\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"High Performance Solution of Tridiagonal Systems on the GPU\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nIn this article, we present PfSolve—a new, performant, cross-platform, and open-source implementation of tridiagonal and bidiagonal matrix solvers for\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nthe ratio of the off-diagonal to diagonal terms) and demonstrates that PfSolve achieves a considerable speedup over vendor solutions on modern HPC\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nBalogh, Tobias S. Flynn, Sylvain Laizet, Gihan R. Mudalige, and István Z. Reguly. 2022. Scalable many-core algorithms for tridiagonal solvers.\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nHwu. 2012. A scalable, numerically stable, high-performance tridiagonal solver using GPUs.\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\n1. High Performance Solution of Tridiagonal Systems on the GPU\\n2. Computing methodologies\\n3. Parallel computing methodologies\\n4. Parallel algorithms\\n5.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.computer.org/csdl/proceedings-article/ipdps/2011/4385a956/12OmNrJiCFY\",\n      \"title\": \"An Auto-tuned Method for Solving Large Tridiagonal Systems on the GPU\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2011-05-01\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Andrew Davidson Yao Zhang [John D. Owens](/csdl/search/default?type=author&givenName=John D.&surname=Owens)\\nDownload PDF SHARE ARTICLE\\nGenerate Citation\\nAbstract\\nWe present a multi-stage method for solving large tridiagonal systems on the GPU. Previously large tridiagonal systems cannot be efficiently solved due to the limitation of on-chip shared memory size. We tackle this problem by splitting the systems into smaller ones and then solving them on-chip.\\nThe multi-stage characteristic of our method, together with various workloads and GPUs of different capabilities, obligates an auto-tuning strategy to carefully select the switch points between computation stages.\\nIn particular, we show two ways to effectively prune the tuning space and thus avoid an impractical exhaustive search: (1) apply algorithmic knowledge to decouple tuning parameters, and (2) estimate search starting points based on GPU architecture parameters.\\nWe demonstrate that auto-tuning is a powerful tool that improves the performance by up to 5x, saves 17% and 32% of execution time on average respectively over static and dynamic tuning, and enables our multi-stage solver to outperform the Intel MKL tridiagonal solver on many parallel tridiagonal systems by 6-11x.\\nLike what you’re reading?\\n* New Tridiagonal Systems Solvers on GPU Architectures 2015 IEEE 22nd International Conference on High Performance Computing (HiPC)\\n* A Hierarchical Tridiagonal System Solver for Heterogenous Supercomputers 2014 5th Workshop on Latest Advances in Scalable Algorithms for Large-Scale Systems (ScalA)\\n* Solving tridiagonal systems on a GPU 2013 20th International Conference on High Performance Computing (HiPC)\\n* A Scalable Tridiagonal Solver for GPUs 2011 International Conference on Parallel Processing\\n* Solving Large Problem Sizes of Index-Digit Algorithms on GPU: FFT and Tridiagonal System Solvers IEEE Transactions on Computers\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://escholarship.org/uc/item/7b441610\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU - eScholarship\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU - eScholarship\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recursive doubling (RD).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.jcohen.name/papers/Zhang_Fast_2009.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recursive doubling (RD).\\nFast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU\\nAbstract We study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recursive doubling (RD).\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea95e7f61ffeAWmqzYp3aSBPPN\"\n}",
